::ENG4_U01_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. What does beneficial mean in this passage? {
~A. Fair across groups#That is closer to equitable.
=B. Producing a positive effect#Correct. Beneficial means helpful or advantageous.
~C. Connected to the issue#That is relevant.
~D. Trustworthy#That is credible.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Which sentence uses equitable correctly? {
~A. The evidence is equitable because it came from a university report.#This describes credibility, not fairness.
~B. The chart is equitable because it appears on the same page as the claim.#This describes placement or relevance, not fairness.
~C. The claim is equitable because it is written in complete sentences.#This confuses conventions with fairness.
=D. The policy is equitable because it distributes support fairly among affected groups.#Correct. Equitable refers to fairness.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which word should describe a source that can be trusted? {
=A. Credible#Correct. Credible means trustworthy.
~B. Relevant#Relevant means connected to the issue.
~C. Beneficial#Beneficial means helpful.
~D. Equitable#Equitable means fair.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Which paraphrase best preserves this distinction? {
~A. Beneficial and equitable both mean the policy is popular.#The passage does not discuss popularity.
~B. A beneficial policy is fair, while an equitable policy is helpful.#This reverses and distorts the distinction.
=C. Beneficial gives a broad positive judgment, while equitable focuses specifically on fairness.#Correct. This keeps the distinction.
~D. Equitable means the policy has no positive effects.#This is not stated and distorts meaning.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which sentence follows standard English conventions? {
~A. Academic vocabulary allow writers to make exact distinctions.#The subject and verb do not agree.
=B. Academic vocabulary allows writers to make exact distinctions.#Correct. The subject and verb agree.
~C. Academic vocabulary allowing writers to make exact distinctions.#This is a fragment.
~D. Academic vocabulary, allow writers to make exact distinctions.#The comma and verb form are incorrect.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Without precise words, an argument may sound confident while leaving the audience unsure about what is being claimed, how evidence supports it, or why the conclusion should be accepted. What does precise most nearly mean? {
=A. Exact and specific#Correct. Precise words make meaning exact.
~B. Emotional and dramatic#The passage focuses on clarity, not drama.
~C. Informal and casual#Academic precision is not informal.
~D. Long and complicated#Length does not equal precision.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which sentence uses relevant correctly? {
~A. The source is relevant because the author has strong credentials.#That describes credibility.
~B. The policy is relevant because it treats all groups fairly.#That describes equity.
=C. The evidence is relevant because it directly addresses the claim being debated.#Correct. Relevant means connected to the issue.
~D. The report is relevant because it was written by a trustworthy agency.#That describes credibility.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Without precise words, an argument may sound confident while leaving the audience unsure about what is being claimed, how evidence supports it, or why the conclusion should be accepted. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. Academic vocabulary makes arguments sound impressive even when they lack evidence.#This changes the passage's meaning.
=B. Precise words help writers and speakers clarify claims, evidence, and conclusions.#Correct. This preserves the passage's meaning.
~C. Academic vocabulary should replace evidence in formal arguments.#The passage does not say vocabulary replaces evidence.
~D. Arguments should sound confident even if the audience is unsure.#This reverses the warning in the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which word means meaningful differences between ideas? {
~A. Claims#Claims are positions presented as true.
=B. Distinctions#Correct. Distinctions are meaningful differences.
~C. Conclusions#Conclusions are final judgments.
~D. Audiences#Audiences are readers or listeners.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Which evaluation is best? {
=A. Equitable is more precise than beneficial when the issue is fairness.#Correct. Equitable specifically names fairness.
~B. Beneficial is always more precise than equitable.#The passage says equitable is more specific about fairness.
~C. Equitable should be used whenever something has any positive effect.#Positive effect is beneficial, not necessarily equitable.
~D. Beneficial and equitable should never appear in the same argument.#The passage does not say this.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which sentence is correctly punctuated? {
~A. Evidence is relevant, when it connects to the issue.#The comma incorrectly separates the clause.
=B. Evidence is relevant when it connects to the issue.#Correct. No comma is needed before the essential when clause.
~C. Evidence, is relevant when it connects to the issue.#The comma incorrectly separates subject and verb.
~D. Evidence is relevant when, it connects to the issue.#The comma incorrectly splits the clause.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Which detail must be preserved in an accurate paraphrase? {
~A. The idea that every public policy is fair#The passage does not say every policy is fair.
=B. The distinction between a broad positive judgment and a fairness claim#Correct. This distinction is central.
~C. The claim that academic vocabulary makes arguments longer#The passage does not say this.
~D. The idea that audiences should accept all conclusions#The passage does not say this.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. A student writes: The evidence is credible because it comes from a peer-reviewed report. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~A. The vocabulary is imprecise because credible means fair.#Credible does not mean fair.
~B. The vocabulary is imprecise because peer review only shows relevance.#Peer review more directly supports trustworthiness.
=C. The vocabulary is precise because peer review supports trustworthiness.#Correct. Credible fits the reason given.
~D. The vocabulary is precise because credible means connected to the issue.#That defines relevant, not credible.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which choice best explains why exact distinctions matter? {
=A. They help an audience understand the writer's specific claim and reasoning.#Correct. The passage connects precision to audience understanding.
~B. They make a paragraph longer regardless of meaning.#The passage does not value length.
~C. They allow a speaker to avoid evidence.#The passage still values evidence.
~D. They guarantee that a conclusion is correct.#Precision helps clarity but does not guarantee truth.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Without precise words, an argument may sound confident while leaving the audience unsure about what is being claimed. Which revision best fixes the sentence? Original: Without precise words an argument may sound confident but leave the audience unsure. {
=A. Without precise words, an argument may sound confident but leave the audience unsure.#Correct. The introductory phrase is followed by a comma.
~B. Without precise words an argument, may sound confident but leave the audience unsure.#This comma separates subject and verb.
~C. Without precise words an argument may sound, confident but leave the audience unsure.#This comma interrupts the adjective phrase.
~D. Without precise, words an argument may sound confident but leave the audience unsure.#This comma incorrectly splits the phrase.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. Which sentence uses precise vocabulary most effectively? {
~A. The evidence is good because it is good for the argument.#This repeats vague language.
=B. The evidence is credible because it comes from a transparent, expert source.#Correct. The sentence uses a precise academic term and explains it.
~C. The evidence is equitable because it is from a website.#Equitable is misused.
~D. The policy is relevant because it is fair to everyone.#Relevant is misused for fairness.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which paraphrase distorts the passage? {
=A. Relevant evidence is trustworthy, while credible evidence is connected to the issue.#Correct. This reverses the meanings.
~B. Relevant evidence relates directly to the issue being discussed.#This is accurate.
~C. Credible evidence is evidence that an audience can trust.#This is accurate.
~D. Evidence can be evaluated for connection and trustworthiness.#This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Which word would best complete this sentence? The tutoring program was blank because it improved graduation rates. {
~A. equitable#Fairness is not the reason given.
=B. beneficial#Correct. Improved graduation rates are a positive effect.
~C. relevant#Relevant means connected to an issue.
~D. credible#Credible means trustworthy.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Without precise words, an argument may sound confident while leaving the audience unsure about what is being claimed, how evidence supports it, or why the conclusion should be accepted. What problem does imprecise vocabulary create? {
~A. It makes the audience read too quickly.#The passage does not discuss reading speed.
~B. It makes the argument impossible to punctuate.#The passage does not make that claim.
=C. It can leave the audience uncertain about claims, evidence, and conclusions.#Correct. This is the stated problem.
~D. It always makes a claim false.#Imprecision weakens clarity but does not always make a claim false.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which sentence is clear and conventional? {
~A. Academic words helps writers makes exact distinctions.#This has agreement errors.
~B. Academic vocabulary help writer's make exact distinction.#This has agreement and apostrophe errors.
=C. Academic vocabulary helps writers make exact distinctions.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~D. Academic vocabulary helping writers to making exact distinctions.#This is not a standard sentence.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. What is the best reason to choose equitable instead of beneficial? {
~A. The writer wants to sound more formal without changing meaning.#The words do change meaning.
=B. The writer wants to focus specifically on fairness.#Correct. Equitable names fairness.
~C. The writer wants to say the evidence is trustworthy.#That would require credible.
~D. The writer wants to say the evidence connects to the issue.#That would require relevant.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Without precise words, an argument may sound confident while leaving the audience unsure about what is being claimed, how evidence supports it, or why the conclusion should be accepted. Which paraphrase is too broad? {
=A. Academic vocabulary helps people communicate.#Correct. This is true but too broad because it omits exact distinctions and audience clarity.
~B. Precise academic words help clarify claims, evidence, and conclusions.#This preserves the key idea.
~C. Exact vocabulary helps writers and speakers show meaningful differences between related ideas.#This is accurate.
~D. Without precise language, an argument can sound confident but remain unclear.#This is accurate.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which sentence shows the strongest command of vocabulary? {
~A. The evidence is good and nice for the topic.#This is vague and informal.
~B. The evidence is equitable because the article is long.#This misuses equitable.
=C. The evidence is relevant because it directly addresses the claim and credible because it comes from a trustworthy agency.#Correct. This distinguishes both qualities accurately.
~D. The evidence is beneficial because it is evidence.#This is circular and imprecise.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which statement best evaluates the phrase "a good source"? {
~A. It is precise because good can mean only credible.#Good is broad and can mean many things.
~B. It is precise because informal words are always clearer.#The passage emphasizes precise academic vocabulary.
=C. It is imprecise because it does not specify whether the source is relevant, credible, or both.#Correct. The phrase needs a more exact academic term.
~D. It is imprecise only because it is misspelled.#The problem is meaning, not spelling.
}
::ENG4_U01_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. Which sentence is grammatically correct and precise? {
=A. Precise vocabulary helps an audience understand the claim, evidence, and conclusion.#Correct. The sentence is conventional and precise.
~B. Precise vocabulary help an audience understands the claim, evidence, and conclusion.#This has agreement and verb-form errors.
~C. Precise vocabulary helps an audience understand the claim evidence and conclusion.#This lacks needed commas in the series.
~D. Precise vocabulary, helps an audience understand claim, evidence, and conclusion.#The comma incorrectly separates subject and verb.
}
